IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `clpa-dates`

-- Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (WA) -- the questions of the person bringing a claim.
--
-- SEAT: the claimant: the person who brings a claim before a court of Western Australia or an arbitrator, where the
-- claim is governed by the law of another Australian State, a Territory or New Zealand (an injury in another State, a
-- contract governed by another State's law). "Does this Act apply to my proceedings?", "Is this time rule a 'limitation
-- law'?", "Whose time limit does the court apply to my claim?", "Am I in time?", "If I ask for more time, how must the
-- court decide?". The court and the arbitrator get no seat: what they would find is asked as a fact.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT. The
-- one duty with a deadline (to commence within the time the governing place's limitation law allows, which s 5 makes
-- part of the law the court applies) is a regulative rule run inside a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: the whole operative Act: s 3 (application), s 4 (court, limitation law), s 5 (characterization of
-- limitation laws), s 6 (exercise of discretion under limitation law). Out: s 1 (short title), s 2 (commencement, used
-- only for its date, 27 May 1994, from the compilation table).
--
-- Edition: Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (WA), Act No. 4 of 1994, reprint as at 8 November 2002 (no
-- amendments since), retrieved 7 September 2026, deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_288.txt. No regulation
-- sets a number this encoding needs.
--
-- Sourced from the Western Australian Legislation website, State of Western Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to a court are INPUTS ("if the courts of that
-- place would ..."). Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is
-- the text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an answer where the text is limited by its own wording is marked
-- "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994: the questions of the person bringing a claim`

-- s 2 and the compilation table: the Act came into operation on 27 May 1994 (Gazette 27 May 1994 p. 2205). Section 3
-- speaks of "the commencement of this section"; the whole Act commenced on that day.
`the commencement of s 3` MEANS `the calendar date` 27 5 1994

-- s 4: "court includes arbitrator". s 5 and s 6 speak of "a court of this State". Fork F2: an arbitrator is taken to be
-- within "a court of this State" when the arbitration is held in Western Australia.
DECLARE `Where my claim is heard` IS ONE OF
    `a court of Western Australia`
    `an arbitrator, in an arbitration held in Western Australia`
    `a court or arbitrator outside Western Australia`

-- s 5 and s 6: "another State, a Territory or New Zealand". Which law governs a claim is decided by the common law of
-- choice of law, which the Act does not state: it is asked as a fact.
DECLARE `The law that governs my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the law of Western Australia`
    `the law of another Australian State`
    `the law of an Australian Territory`
    `the law of New Zealand`
    `the law of a country other than Australia or New Zealand`

GIVEN `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `another State, a Territory or New Zealand` `the law` IF
        `the law` EQUALS `the law of another Australian State`
    OR  `the law` EQUALS `the law of an Australian Territory`
    OR  `the law` EQUALS `the law of New Zealand`

GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a court of this State` `the forum` IF
        `the forum` EQUALS `a court of Western Australia`
    OR  `the forum` EQUALS `an arbitrator, in an arbitration held in Western Australia`

§§ `s 3 -- Application`

-- s 3(1): "This Act extends to a cause of action that arose before the commencement of this section but does not apply
-- to proceedings instituted before the commencement of this section."
-- s 3(2): "This Act does not apply in relation to New Zealand unless a declaration that it does so apply is made by the
-- proclamation made under section 2 or by a proclamation made under this subsection."
-- s 3(3): "If the substantive law of New Zealand is to govern a claim before a court of this State and proceedings have
-- been instituted on the claim before a declaration is made as referred to in subsection (2), this Act does not apply
-- to those proceedings."
-- All three "no" answers are the text's no: a flat negative ("does not apply") with "unless" and "if" conditions.
DECLARE `Does this Act apply to my proceedings` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Act applies to these proceedings (s 3), even if the cause of action arose before 27 May 1994 (s 3(1))`
    `no: the proceedings were instituted before 27 May 1994 (s 3(1), the text's no)`
    `no: New Zealand law governs the claim and no proclamation has declared that the Act applies in relation to New Zealand (s 3(2), the text's no)`
    `no: New Zealand law governs the claim and the proceedings were instituted before the declaration for New Zealand was made (s 3(3), the text's no)`

-- The day the proceedings were (or will be) instituted: the day given, or today if they have not been instituted yet
-- (they can be instituted no earlier than today).
GIVEN `instituted` IS A BOOLEAN, `instituted on` IS A DATE, `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day of institution` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` MEANS
    IF `instituted` THEN `instituted on` ELSE `today`

@export Does the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (WA) apply to my proceedings (s 3)? It applies to proceedings instituted on or after 27 May 1994, even on a cause of action that arose earlier. Where New Zealand law governs the claim, it applies only once a proclamation has declared that it applies in relation to New Zealand, and not to proceedings instituted before that declaration.
GIVEN `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim` @desc Which place's law governs your claim (its substantive law, as a court would decide under the rules of choice of law)
      `instituted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already instituted proceedings on the claim (filed in court, or commenced the arbitration)
      `instituted on` IS A DATE @desc The day you instituted them (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet instituted proceedings)
      `New Zealand declared` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a proclamation has declared that the Act applies in relation to New Zealand (s 3(2)); only matters if New Zealand law governs
      `declared on` IS A DATE @desc The day that declaration was made (if none, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Does this Act apply to my proceedings`
`s 3 -- does this Act apply to my proceedings` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` `New Zealand declared` `declared on` MEANS
    IF `the day` `falls before` `the commencement of s 3`
    THEN `no: the proceedings were instituted before 27 May 1994 (s 3(1), the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `the law` EQUALS `the law of New Zealand` AND (NOT `New Zealand declared`)
         THEN `no: New Zealand law governs the claim and no proclamation has declared that the Act applies in relation to New Zealand (s 3(2), the text's no)`
         ELSE IF `the law` EQUALS `the law of New Zealand` AND (`the day` `falls before` `declared on`)
              THEN `no: New Zealand law governs the claim and the proceedings were instituted before the declaration for New Zealand was made (s 3(3), the text's no)`
              ELSE `yes: the Act applies to these proceedings (s 3), even if the cause of action arose before 27 May 1994 (s 3(1))`
    WHERE
        `the day` MEANS `the day of institution` `instituted` `instituted on` `today`

GIVEN `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim`
      `instituted` IS A BOOLEAN
      `instituted on` IS A DATE
      `today` IS A DATE
      `New Zealand declared` IS A BOOLEAN
      `declared on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act applies` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` `New Zealand declared` `declared on` IF
    `s 3 -- does this Act apply to my proceedings` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` `New Zealand declared` `declared on`
        EQUALS `yes: the Act applies to these proceedings (s 3), even if the cause of action arose before 27 May 1994 (s 3(1))`

§§ `s 4 -- Interpretation`

-- s 4: "limitation law means a law that provides for the limitation or exclusion of any liability or the barring of a
-- right of action in respect of a claim by reference to the time when a proceeding on, or the arbitration of, the claim
-- is commenced." A "means" definition: when either element fails, the answer is the text's no (JUST FALSE as a BOOLEAN
-- FALSE). Plain gloss: a time limit for suing, whether it kills the claim, the remedy or the liability.
-- "unless the contrary intention appears": no contrary intention appears in ss 3, 5 or 6 (fork F1).
@export Is the time rule I face a "limitation law" within the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (WA) (s 4)? A limitation law is a law that limits or excludes a liability, or bars a right of action, on a claim by reference to the time when a proceeding on the claim, or its arbitration, is commenced.
GIVEN `limits or bars` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the rule limits or excludes a liability, or bars a right of action, on your claim (for example, says no action may be brought, or that the right is extinguished)
      `by the time of commencing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it does so by reference to the time when a proceeding on the claim, or the arbitration of the claim, is commenced (for example, "within 3 years after the cause of action accrued")
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 4 -- is it a limitation law` `limits or bars` `by the time of commencing` IF
        `limits or bars`
    AND `by the time of commencing`

§§ `s 5 -- Characterization of limitation laws`

-- s 5: "If the substantive law of another place being another State, a Territory or New Zealand, is to govern a claim
-- before a court of this State, a limitation law of that place is to be regarded as part of that substantive law and
-- applied accordingly by the court."
-- The "if" failing is the text's no (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05): s 5 does not make the other place's limitation law
-- apply. The Act then says nothing about which time limit applies: the answer says so, and does not answer it.
DECLARE `What s 5 says about the time limit on my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the limitation law of the place whose law governs the claim applies to it, as part of that place's substantive law (s 5)`
    `not under this Act: it does not apply to these proceedings (s 3, the text's no)`
    `s 5 does not apply: the claim is not before a court or arbitrator of Western Australia (the text's no: its "if" fails); this Act does not say which time limit applies`
    `s 5 does not apply: the claim is not governed by the law of another State, a Territory or New Zealand (the text's no: its "if" fails); this Act does not say which time limit applies`

@export Whose time limit applies to my claim before a Western Australian court or arbitrator (Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (WA) s 5)? If the law of another State, a Territory or New Zealand governs the claim, that place's limitation law is treated as part of its substantive law and the court applies it, not Western Australia's.
GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard` @desc Where your claim is (or will be) heard
      `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim` @desc Which place's law governs your claim (its substantive law, as a court would decide under the rules of choice of law)
      `instituted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already instituted proceedings on the claim (filed in court, or commenced the arbitration)
      `instituted on` IS A DATE @desc The day you instituted them (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet instituted proceedings)
      `New Zealand declared` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a proclamation has declared that the Act applies in relation to New Zealand (s 3(2)); only matters if New Zealand law governs
      `declared on` IS A DATE @desc The day that declaration was made (if none, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `What s 5 says about the time limit on my claim`
`s 5 -- whose time limit applies to my claim` `the forum` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` `New Zealand declared` `declared on` MEANS
    IF NOT `the Act applies` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` `New Zealand declared` `declared on`
    THEN `not under this Act: it does not apply to these proceedings (s 3, the text's no)`
    ELSE IF NOT `a court of this State` `the forum`
         THEN `s 5 does not apply: the claim is not before a court or arbitrator of Western Australia (the text's no: its "if" fails); this Act does not say which time limit applies`
         ELSE IF NOT `another State, a Territory or New Zealand` `the law`
              THEN `s 5 does not apply: the claim is not governed by the law of another State, a Territory or New Zealand (the text's no: its "if" fails); this Act does not say which time limit applies`
              ELSE `the limitation law of the place whose law governs the claim applies to it, as part of that place's substantive law (s 5)`

§§ `s 5 -- Commencing within the time that limitation law allows`

-- The claimant's duty with a deadline. The Act imposes no time limit of its own; s 5 makes the governing place's
-- limitation law part of the law the court applies, so the claimant must commence within the time that law allows. The
-- deadline is counted in days from the day time began to run under that law to the last day that law allows; both days
-- are the claimant's facts, read from that law (its counting rules are not this Act's: fork F4).
DECLARE `Claimant` IS ONE OF `the claimant`
DECLARE `Claim step` IS ONE OF `commence the proceeding or arbitration`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claimant` `Claim step`
`s 5 -- commence within the time the governing limitation law allows` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MUST `commence the proceeding or arbitration`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "not commenced by the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows: s 5 makes that law part of the law the court applies"

-- Runs the rule over what happened. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH, so
-- FULFILLED means in time and anything else late. It takes no DEONTIC from outside and is not exported.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 5 -- commencing on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 5 -- commence within the time the governing limitation law allows` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the claimant` `commence the proceeding or arbitration` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Am I in time` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you commenced on or before the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows (s 5)`
    `still in time: you have not commenced, and the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows has not passed (s 5)`
    `out of time under the limitation law of the governing place (s 5): only a discretion that law gives the court can help (see s 6)`
    `this Act does not make another place's time limit apply on these facts (s 3 or s 5, the text's no)`

@export Am I in time under the limitation law that the court must apply to my claim (Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (WA) s 5)? Where s 5 applies, you must commence the proceeding or arbitration by the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows.
GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard` @desc Where your claim is (or will be) heard
      `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim` @desc Which place's law governs your claim (its substantive law, as a court would decide under the rules of choice of law)
      `instituted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already instituted proceedings on the claim (filed in court, or commenced the arbitration)
      `instituted on` IS A DATE @desc The day you instituted them (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet instituted proceedings)
      `New Zealand declared` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a proclamation has declared that the Act applies in relation to New Zealand (s 3(2)); only matters if New Zealand law governs
      `declared on` IS A DATE @desc The day that declaration was made (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `time began on` IS A DATE @desc The day time began to run against you under the governing place's limitation law (usually when the cause of action accrued)
      `last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day on which that limitation law lets you commence the proceeding or arbitration
GIVETH A `Am I in time`
`s 5 -- am I in time` `the forum` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` `New Zealand declared` `declared on` `time began on` `last day` MEANS
    IF NOT (`s 5 -- whose time limit applies to my claim` `the forum` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` `New Zealand declared` `declared on`
                EQUALS `the limitation law of the place whose law governs the claim applies to it, as part of that place's substantive law (s 5)`)
    THEN `this Act does not make another place's time limit apply on these facts (s 3 or s 5, the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `instituted`
         THEN IF `s 5 -- commencing on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day commenced`
              THEN `in time: you commenced on or before the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows (s 5)`
              ELSE `out of time under the limitation law of the governing place (s 5): only a discretion that law gives the court can help (see s 6)`
         ELSE IF `last day` `falls before` `today`
              THEN `out of time under the limitation law of the governing place (s 5): only a discretion that law gives the court can help (see s 6)`
              ELSE `still in time: you have not commenced, and the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows has not passed (s 5)`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `time began on` `to` `last day`
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `time began on` `to` `instituted on`
        -- A proceeding commenced before time began to run is counted as commenced on day 0 (in time).
        `the day commenced` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

§§ `s 6 -- Exercise of discretion under limitation law`

-- s 6: "Where a court of this State exercises a discretion conferred under a limitation law of a place being another
-- State, a Territory or New Zealand that discretion, as far as practicable, is to be exercised in the manner in which it
-- is exercised in comparable cases by the courts of that place."
-- The "where" failing is the text's no (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05). "As far as practicable" is a limit in the section's
-- own wording: where following that place's courts is not practicable, s 6 says no more (NOTHING-kind). How the courts of
-- that place exercise the discretion in comparable cases is a judgment, asked as a fact.
DECLARE `How the court must use a discretion under that limitation law` IS ONE OF
    `as the courts of that place do in comparable cases, and on these facts they would exercise it in your favour (s 6)`
    `as the courts of that place do in comparable cases, and on these facts they would not exercise it in your favour (s 6)`
    `s 6 asks the court to follow that place's courts only as far as practicable; here it is not practicable, and s 6 says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 6 does not arise: that limitation law gives the court no discretion that you have asked it to exercise (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `s 6 does not arise: not a court or arbitrator of Western Australia, or not the limitation law of another State, a Territory or New Zealand (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `s 6 does not arise under this Act: it does not apply to these proceedings (s 3, the text's no)`

@export If I ask the Western Australian court to use a discretion under the other place's limitation law (for example, to extend time), how must it decide (Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1994 (WA) s 6)? As far as practicable, the court exercises the discretion as the courts of that State, Territory or New Zealand exercise it in comparable cases.
GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard` @desc Where your claim is (or will be) heard
      `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim` @desc The place whose limitation law gives the discretion (usually the place whose law governs your claim)
      `instituted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already instituted proceedings on the claim (filed in court, or commenced the arbitration)
      `instituted on` IS A DATE @desc The day you instituted them (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet instituted proceedings)
      `New Zealand declared` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a proclamation has declared that the Act applies in relation to New Zealand (s 3(2)); only matters if New Zealand law governs
      `declared on` IS A DATE @desc The day that declaration was made (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `a discretion asked for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that place's limitation law gives the court a discretion (for example, to extend the limitation period) and you have asked the court to exercise it
      `practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is practicable for the court to follow how the courts of that place exercise the discretion (for example, whether there are comparable cases from those courts to follow)
      `that place's courts would favour me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the courts of that place, in cases comparable to yours, would exercise the discretion in your favour
GIVETH A `How the court must use a discretion under that limitation law`
`s 6 -- how must the court use a discretion under that limitation law` `the forum` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` `New Zealand declared` `declared on` `a discretion asked for` `practicable` `that place's courts would favour me` MEANS
    IF NOT `the Act applies` `the law` `instituted` `instituted on` `today` `New Zealand declared` `declared on`
    THEN `s 6 does not arise under this Act: it does not apply to these proceedings (s 3, the text's no)`
    ELSE IF (NOT `a court of this State` `the forum`) OR (NOT `another State, a Territory or New Zealand` `the law`)
         THEN `s 6 does not arise: not a court or arbitrator of Western Australia, or not the limitation law of another State, a Territory or New Zealand (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
         ELSE IF NOT `a discretion asked for`
              THEN `s 6 does not arise: that limitation law gives the court no discretion that you have asked it to exercise (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
              ELSE IF NOT `practicable`
                   THEN `s 6 asks the court to follow that place's courts only as far as practicable; here it is not practicable, and s 6 says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
                   ELSE IF `that place's courts would favour me`
                        THEN `as the courts of that place do in comparable cases, and on these facts they would exercise it in your favour (s 6)`
                        ELSE `as the courts of that place do in comparable cases, and on these facts they would not exercise it in your favour (s 6)`
